Build FastCap 2.0 (MIT, 1992; ediloren/FastCap2 `master`) with a 2020s C
compiler -- issue #2015, pairing #4 of cross-validation tracking issue #2007.

Applied by scripts/install-fastcap.sh with `patch -p1` against the pinned
source archive named in that script; `patch` fails closed if either hunk
does not apply exactly, so a silently-drifted upstream can never be built
and compared against. Both hunks are build fixes only -- nothing here
touches the discretisation, the multipole expansion, the kernel, or the
solve, so the numbers this build produces are FastCap's own.

Deliberately NOT taken from ediloren/FastCap2's `WRCad` branch, which
already carries equivalent fixes: that branch is distributed under MIT's
original 1990 "internal, noncommercial purposes" license, while `master`
carries the 2003 relicensing ("use, copy, modify, sell and/or distribute
... for any purpose ... without royalty"). This repo is MIT and public, so
the permissively-licensed branch plus this patch is the right pairing --
see docs/design/fastcap-oracle.md's "Licensing" section.

--- a/src/mulGlobal.h
+++ b/src/mulGlobal.h
@@ -72,10 +72,18 @@
   - MALLOC() used when memory can be anything
     core should be malloc() or ualloc()
 ***********************************************************************/
-/* #define CALCORE(NUM, TYPE) calloc((unsigned)(NUM),sizeof(TYPE)) */
-#define CALCORE(NUM, TYPE) ualloc((unsigned)(NUM)*sizeof(TYPE))
-/* #define MALCORE malloc */
-#define MALCORE ualloc
+/* klayout-tools (issue #2015): use the C library allocator, not FastCap's
+   own sbrk()-based `ualloc`.  `ualloc` returns a `char *` but is never
+   prototyped, so on LP64 every allocation is truncated to the implicitly
+   declared `int` return type and the first dereference segfaults; sbrk()
+   itself is also deprecated on modern systems.  This is the same switch
+   Whiteley Research made in their own FastCap branch ("Default now is to
+   use calloc/malloc rather than ualloc.  The sbrk function is deprecated
+   in many operating systems").  The sbrk() stub keeps DUMPRSS's memory
+   report compiling; it is diagnostic output only. */
+#define CALCORE(NUM, TYPE) calloc((unsigned)(NUM),sizeof(TYPE))
+#define MALCORE malloc
+#define sbrk(x) 0
 
 /* counts of memory usage by multipole matrix type */
 extern long memcount;
--- a/src/mulSetup.c
+++ b/src/mulSetup.c
@@ -35,6 +35,21 @@
 
 #include "mulGlobal.h"
 
+/* klayout-tools (issue #2015): forward-declare this file's own static
+   helpers.  Each is called above its K&R definition, which creates an
+   implicit *extern* declaration; the later `static` definition is then a
+   hard error ("static declaration of 'X' follows non-static declaration")
+   on every C compiler since GCC 9.  Declaring them unprototyped keeps them
+   byte-compatible with the K&R definitions below. */
+static int placeq();
+static int indexkid();
+static int getnbrs();
+static int linkcubes();
+static int setMaxq();
+static int getAllInter();
+static int set_vector_masks();
+
+
 cube *cstack[1024];		/* Stack used in several routines. */
 
